Below is a self-contained Rust function that returns the indices of the N largest values from a slice by accessing elements by their positions. It does not depend on any external crates, but the implementation organizes the selection by index (so it matches the "access elements based on their positions" style). If you have a `top-n-indices` crate with a compatible API you can replace the inner selection with the crate call — I show a small comment where that would go.

The function is generic over types that implement PartialOrd + Copy. It handles n > len by returning as many indices as available, and treats incomparable comparisons (e.g., NaN) as "smaller" so they don't pollute top results.

Example usage is included.

```rust
/// Return the indices of the `n` largest elements in `values`.
/// The returned indices are ordered from largest value to smallest value.
/// If `n >= values.len()`, returns all indices sorted by value descending.
///
/// Generic over T: PartialOrd + Copy. Incomparable comparisons (None from partial_cmp)
/// are treated as "smaller" than any comparable value.
///
/// This function is self-contained and does not require external crates.
/// If you have a `top-n-indices` crate with an API that accepts an index accessor,
/// you can replace the inner loop with that crate's call.
pub fn top_n_indices_by_position<T: PartialOrd + Copy>(values: &[T], n: usize) -> Vec<usize> {
    if n == 0 || values.is_empty() {
        return Vec::new();
    }

    let want = n.min(values.len());
    // `top` will contain indices sorted by value descending (largest first).
    let mut top: Vec<usize> = Vec::with_capacity(want);

    for i in 0..values.len() {
        if top.len() < want {
            // Insert i and then keep sorted
            top.push(i);
            top.sort_by(|&a, &b| {
                values[b]
                    .partial_cmp(&values[a])
                    .unwrap_or(std::cmp::Ordering::Less)
            });
        } else {
            // Compare with the smallest among current top (last element)
            let last_idx = *top.last().unwrap();
            let cmp = values[i].partial_cmp(&values[last_idx]);
            let greater = match cmp {
                Some(core::cmp::Ordering::Greater) => true,
                Some(core::cmp::Ordering::Equal) => false,
                Some(core::cmp::Ordering::Less) => false,
                None => false, // treat incomparable (e.g. NaN) as not greater
            };
            if greater {
                // find insertion position (first j where values[i] >= values[j])
                let pos = top
                    .iter()
                    .position(|&j| {
                        values[i]
                            .partial_cmp(&values[j])
                            .unwrap_or(std::cmp::Ordering::Less)
                            != std::cmp::Ordering::Less
                    })
                    .unwrap_or(top.len());
                top.insert(pos, i);
                top.pop(); // keep size == want
            }
        }
    }

    top
}

