Below is a self-contained, generic Rust function that reorders a mutable list (lst0) so its elements follow the ordering defined by a second, already-sorted list (lst1). It is optimized for cases where the reference list (lst1) can be very large by switching between two lookup strategies:

- If lst1 is not dramatically larger than lst0, it builds a HashMap of item -> index (fast lookups).
- If lst1 is much larger than lst0, it performs a binary search into the sorted reference list for each element of lst0 (much lower memory usage; O(n0 log n1) time).

Elements from lst0 that are not present in lst1 are placed after those that are present, preserving their relative order (stable ordering).

The function is fully generic; T must implement Ord + Hash + Eq + Clone so both binary search and hash-based lookup are supported.

Example usage is included.

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::hash::Hash;

/// Reorder `lst0` in-place so its elements follow the ordering in `lst1`.
///
/// - `lst1` is assumed to be already sorted.
/// - Elements present in `lst1` are ordered according to their first occurrence in `lst1`.
/// - Elements in `lst0` not found in `lst1` are placed after the matched elements,
///   and their relative order is preserved (stable).
///
/// This function chooses between two strategies:
/// - If the reference list is not vastly larger than `lst0`, it builds a HashMap of
///   element -> index for O(1) lookup per element (faster but uses extra memory).
/// - If the reference list is much larger than `lst0`, it uses binary search into
///   the sorted `lst1` for each element (uses little extra memory).
pub fn reorder_follow_ref<T>(lst0: &mut [T], lst1: &[T])
where
    T: Ord + Eq + Hash + Clone,
{
    if lst0.is_empty() || lst1.is_empty() {
        // Nothing to reorder (if lst1 is empty, we keep lst0 as-is).
        return;
    }

    // Heuristic threshold; tune as desired.
    // If lst1.len() <= THRESHOLD * lst0.len(), build a HashMap; otherwise use binary search.
    const THRESHOLD: usize = 8;

    if lst1.len() <= THRESHOLD.saturating_mul(lst0.len().max(1)) {
        // Build a map from value -> first index in lst1 (so duplicates in lst1 use first occurrence).
        let mut map: HashMap<T, usize> = HashMap::with_capacity(lst1.len());
        for (i, v) in lst1.iter().enumerate() {
            // Insert only the first occurrence to define ordering deterministically.
            map.entry(v.clone()).or_insert(i);
        }

        // Stable sort lst0 by the index found in the map; missing items get usize::MAX (placed at end).
        lst0.sort_by_key(|x| map.get(x).cloned().unwrap_or(usize::MAX));
    } else {
        // Use binary_search on the sorted lst1 for each element.
        // For duplicates in lst1 we find the first occurrence by walking left.
        lst0.sort_by_key(|x| {
            match lst1.binary_search(x) {
                Ok(mut idx) => {
                    // Move to first equal entry (in case of duplicates)
                    while idx > 0 && &lst1[idx - 1] == x {
                        idx -= 1;
                    }
                    idx
                }
                Err(_) => usize::MAX,
            }
        });
    }
}

fn main() {
    // Example 1: integers
    let mut a = vec![3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2];
    let ref_sorted = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]; // sorted reference
    reorder_follow_ref(&mut a, &ref_sorted);
    println!("Reordered a: {:?}", a);
    // Output: Reordered a: [1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 9]  (9 at end because 9 maps to last index in ref_sorted)

    // Example 2: strings with some missing values
    let mut names = vec![
        "charlie".to_string(),
        "alice".to_string(),
        "bob".to_string(),
        "zara".to_string(),
    ];
    let ref_names = vec![
        "alice".to_string(),
        "bob".to_string(),
        "charlie".to_string(),
    ];
    reorder_follow_ref(&mut names, &ref_names);
    println!("Reordered names: {:?}", names);
    // Output: Reordered names: ["alice", "bob", "charlie", "zara"]
}
```

